    "slug": "somalia-marks-72nd-anniversary-of-national-flag-design",
    "summary": "Somali communities worldwide commemorate the 1954 creation of the blue flag with the white star, a symbol of national unity and independence.",
    "content": "On October 12, 1954, Mohamed Awale Liban designed the light blue flag with a white five-pointed star at its center. This design was later adopted as the official emblem of the Somali Republic upon independence in 1960. The flag was first raised in Hargeisa on June 26, 1960, marking the independence of British Somaliland. During that event, the renowned poet Abdullahi Sultan Timacadde recited a famous poem that included the lines: \"Sarreeyow ma nusqaamow / Aan siduu yahay eegnee / Kaana siib, kanna saar.\" The poem reflects on the flag's symbolism and the unity it represents. The blue color is often said to represent the sky and the Indian Ocean, while the white star symbolizes the five regions where Somalis live: Djibouti, the Ogaden, the North Eastern Province of Kenya, and the former Italian and British Somaliland territories.\n\nMohamed Awale Liban, a Somali scholar and nationalist, created the flag as a symbol of unity and independence. The flag's adoption came two years after its design, when the two Somaliland territories united to form the Somali Republic. The five-pointed star has been a subject of debate, with some questioning whether it represents the five Somali-inhabited regions or the five pillars of Islam. However, the prevailing interpretation is that it stands for the five regions.\n\nThe flag holds deep significance for the Somali diaspora, serving as a powerful emblem of national identity and pride. It is displayed at cultural events, independence day celebrations, and community gatherings around the world. For many in the diaspora, the flag represents a connection to their homeland and a shared heritage that transcends clan and regional differences.\n\nThis anniversary comes amid ongoing challenges in Somalia, including political tensions and security concerns. Yet the flag remains a unifying symbol, reminding Somalis everywhere of their common history and aspirations. The diaspora continues to play a vital role in supporting their homeland through remittances and advocacy.\n\nAs Somalis mark this day, they reflect on the flag's enduring message of unity and sovereignty. The design by Mohamed Awale Liban continues to inspire hope for a peaceful and prosperous future.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/12/maalinta-calanka-soomaaliyeed/",
